package cajeta.lang;

/**
 * Policy for what to do when an encoding conversion can't represent
 * a codepoint in the target encoding (or vice versa — bytes don't
 * decode into the source encoding cleanly).
 *
 *  - FAIL   : throw `EncodingException` carrying enough detail to
 *             pinpoint the bad codepoint / byte position. Default.
 *  - REPAIR : substitute with the encoding's standard replacement
 *             marker (U+FFFD for UTF targets, '?' for ASCII /
 *             Latin-1 / Windows-1252, codec-specified for CJK).
 *
 * No IGNORE policy. Silent data loss is strictly worse than
 * substitution because the user has zero hint anything happened —
 * substitution at least produces detectable output. If the rare
 * "I genuinely want characters dropped" case shows up, decode-
 * iterate + filter codepoints manually before re-encoding.
 *
 * ## Usage
 *
 * Pair an `EncodingErrorPolicy` with an `Encoding` at every
 * bytes ↔ text boundary: the `Encoding` selects the target byte
 * representation, while this policy decides what happens to codepoints
 * that representation can't carry. Under `FAIL` an unrepresentable
 * codepoint raises `EncodingException`; under `REPAIR` it is swapped
 * for that encoding's replacement marker and the conversion continues.
 * The `String` conversion methods that accept this policy land in a
 * later phase, so it is named in prose here rather than shown in a call.
 *
 * Cajeta stdlib parsing-policy enums follow this { FAIL, REPAIR }
 * shape across all parsing APIs (encoding, JSON, number parsing,
 * etc.). The semantics of REPAIR differ per domain — substitute
 * for encoding, lenient-syntax for JSON, saturate for numeric — but
 * the *shape* is uniform. See docs/stdlib/lang/String.md §
 * Parsing convention.
 */
public enum EncodingErrorPolicy {
    /**
     * Abort the conversion and throw `EncodingException` on the first
     * codepoint/byte that can't be represented. The exception pinpoints
     * the offending position so the caller can react. This is the default
     * policy — bad data is surfaced, never silently mangled.
     */
    FAIL,

    /**
     * Replace each unrepresentable codepoint/byte with the target
     * encoding's standard replacement marker (`U+FFFD` for UTF targets,
     * `'?'` for ASCII / Latin-1 / Windows-1252, codec-specified for CJK)
     * and continue. Produces detectable output instead of throwing.
     */
    REPAIR
}
